+// Recovery, second round. Matching the red view against the cyan one is
+// matching two different colour channels, and on colour edges they disagree:
+// the edge flips (a grey wall to red paint brightens in red, darkens in
+// green) or all but vanishes in one. There the true match scored worst, a
+// wrong one won, and its colour was borrowed: most blobs on coloured objects
+// were never matched right at any stage. So: recover both eyes' colour
+// roughly with the first round's disparities (this 1/4 level; own channels
+// as seen, the other view's borrowed), and match again on the two recovered
+// LUMINANCES -- the same kind of picture in both eyes, whose edges agree.
+// Target 1/4: .r the left eye's luminance, .g the right eye's. dispTex: the
+// first round's smoothed disparity; srcTex: the 1/4 anaglyph.
+float4 PSRecLuma(VSOut i) : SV_Target
+{
+    const float2 uv = i.uv;
+    const float4 d = dispTex.SampleLevel(samp, uv, 0.0);
+    const float3 c  = srcTex.SampleLevel(samp, uv, 0.0).rgb;
+    const float3 cL = srcTex.SampleLevel(samp, float2(uv.x + d.r, uv.y), 0.0).rgb;   // the right view where the left eye's point is
+    const float3 cR = srcTex.SampleLevel(samp, float2(uv.x + d.g, uv.y), 0.0).rgb;   // the left view where the right eye's point is
+    const float3 mL = anaFilter(float3(1, 1, 1), g_anaCombo, 0), mR = anaFilter(float3(1, 1, 1), g_anaCombo, 1);
+    const float3 L = c * mL + cL * (1.0 - mL);
+    const float3 R = c * mR + cR * (1.0 - mR);
+    const float3 Yw = float3(0.299, 0.587, 0.114);
+    return float4(dot(L, Yw), dot(R, Yw), 0, 1);
+}
